WEBVTT

00:00.560 --> 00:04.640
Most EEG foundation models learn by hiding
parts of the signal.

00:04.640 --> 00:06.640
But which parts should they hide?

00:08.420 --> 00:10.080
Take masked autoencoders:

00:10.080 --> 00:12.500
the network sees parts of the EEG and

00:12.500 --> 00:15.020
rebuilds the raw signal of the hidden
pieces.

00:17.000 --> 00:20.860
JEPA does the same, but predicts how a
teacher network would encode them.

00:22.300 --> 00:26.220
They both rely on a mask. So which mask
works best?

00:27.540 --> 00:32.960
We describe every mask with three
parameters: radius, length and ratio.

00:33.480 --> 00:36.220
With one electrode at a time, you have
random patches.

00:36.220 --> 00:39.620
With all electrodes at once, you have
whole time segments.

00:39.660 --> 00:43.100
If you stretch a block over a whole
window, you hide an entire channel.

00:43.100 --> 00:46.500
And in between, you have spatio-temporal
blocks.

00:46.680 --> 00:50.520
With three numbers, we cover all the masks
from the literature.

00:51.880 --> 00:57.560
In this study, we trained 58 models that
differ only in mask and pretext.

00:57.980 --> 01:01.640
We evaluated them on OpenEEGBench.

01:03.160 --> 01:07.300
The winner is the same for both masked
autoencoders and JEPA:

01:07.300 --> 01:10.400
blocks of 2 seconds with a 9 cm radius.

01:10.940 --> 01:13.300
Masks fail when they are too local,

01:13.300 --> 01:15.860
because the neighbouring electrodes give
away the answer.

01:15.860 --> 01:21.060
They also fail when they are too global,
leaving only the time to predict from,

01:21.160 --> 01:24.020
or, in most cases, when they are too long.

01:25.680 --> 01:28.840
Yet the winning configuration is quite
robust,

01:28.840 --> 01:32.440
with multiple masks statistically tied
with the best.

01:33.910 --> 01:35.400
How do we explain these results?

01:35.400 --> 01:39.740
We observed that EEG is highly redundant
across electrodes,

01:39.740 --> 01:41.840
but much less across time.

01:41.900 --> 01:46.800
So a good mask should hide enough
neighbours to make the task hard,

01:46.800 --> 01:49.260
while staying short in time.

01:52.520 --> 01:57.820
All weights are available on Hugging Face,
the paper is on arXiv.
